---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi3_40",
  "series": "Band VI 3 (1672-1676); durchgesehener Nachdruck 1980",
  "seq": 40,
  "text": "[[403.40]] <b>40. De imperfectione analyseos </b> <span>403.0;0:63:040:73       :L:l^</span>.\n[[403.12]] P. Fabri in Methodo: inventum Analyticorum priorum Aristotelis nulli Algebrae\n[[403.13]] postponendum.\n[[403.14]] P. Fabri in Methodo ait regulam Galilaei (aequali tempore acquiri motu naturaliter\n[[403.15]] accelerato spatium idem quod acquiritur motu aequabili, cuius velocitas sit\n[[403.16]] dupla maximae et minimae prioris) accidere etiam si spatium acquisitum sit summa\n[[403.17]] terminorum progressionis Arithmeticae simplicis, non 1. 3. 5. 7. etc. sed 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6.\n[[403.18]] 7. etc. At vero hoc mihi videtur adeo non obstare Galilaeo, ut ex eius sententia\n[[403.19]] sequatur, nam, si Triangulum illud ex velocitatibus continue crescentibus in altitudinem\n[[403.20]] ductis descriptum resolvas in lineas usque basi parallelas et altitudinem in\n[[403.21]] partes minimas seu puncta, lineae crescent, ut numeri progressionis Arithmeticae\n[[403.22]] simplicis. At si dividas altitudinem in lineas aequales seu partes aequales finitas,\n[[403.23]] spatia ab applicatis seu basi parallelis abscissa crescent ut numeri progressionis Arithmeticae\n[[403.24]] simplicis. Quare velocitates quolibet momento crescent, ut numeri progressionis\n[[403.25]] Arithmeticae simplicis, at non spatia aequalibus temporibus absoluta.\n[[404.1]] P. Fabri ait Savotium esse suo judicio optimum eorum qui de Coloribus scripsere.\n[[404.3]] De Imperfectione Analyseos, deque eius supplemento per Synthesin.\n[[404.4]] Inventio fit per analysin, ubicunque ad inveniendum nihil aliud assumitur quam\n[[404.5]] ipsum problema aut theorema datum, quod in prima sua Elementa resolutum praebet\n[[404.6]] nobis solutionem. Inventio per synthesin est cum alia praecognita requiruntur. Cognitio\n[[404.7]] perfecta omnium rerum per analysin non nisi eius est cui omnium rerum synthesis\n[[404.8]] debetur, id est Dei. Analysis impeditur in rebus Physicis, quia experimenta\n[[404.9]] ignoramus, nec nisi aut casu aut multo impenso tempore invenimus. Quare in physicis\n[[404.10]] nihil nobis ita datum est, ut clare distincteque cognosci sit in nostra potestate.\n[[404.11]] Adde idiosyncrasias et varietates subjectorum. In agendis omnibus generaliter Analysis\n[[404.12]] plerumque non potest adhiberi, quia multi nimis temporis res est, et infinita sunt\n[[404.13]] quae in calculum adhiberi debent, adde aliud malum, quod rigore methodi analyticae\n[[404.14]] multa adhiberemus in calculum quae res postea ostenderet esse aliena, haec autem ab\n[[404.15]] homine promto et ingenioso statim ab initio rejiciuntur. Imaginatione quoque forti,\n[[404.16]] seu memoria opus est, sed maxime reminiscentia quae rerum similium admoneat.\n[[404.17]] Alioqui semper libris seu catalogis opus foret. In quaestionibus politicis generalibus,\n[[404.18]] sed omnium maxime in Iuris quaestionibus certissima perfectissimaque est analysis,\n[[404.19]] item in quibusdam Metaphysicis. Quod ad Arithmeticam et Geometriam in iis sunt\n[[404.20]] quaedam analysis impatientia, in illa ob multitudinem, in hac ob infinitatem rerum\n[[404.21]] considerandarum, est enim in Geometria, Arithmetica infiniti. In primis autem scientia\n[[404.22]] serierum seu progressionum analysin recusat. In Geometria analysi non invenientur\n[[404.23]] nisi forte per Arithmeticam infinitorum prima Elementa, ut 47. Euclidis, item\n[[404.24]] 3 Angulos esse 2 rectis aequales in triangulo nemo opinor per analysin seu calculum,\n[[404.25]] sed par un hasard quasi, conjectis in hunc ducendarum linearum modum oculis\n[[404.26]] invenerit. Hoc ergo inveniendum ad definitionem Analyseos supra positam, ut\n[[404.27]] in ea nihil inveniatur casu.\n[[405.1]] In ipsa Analysi Geometrarum defectus hi sunt, primum in ipsis speciebus defectus\n[[405.2]] est in divisione per binomia, malum enim est quod si multiplices per binomia\n[[405.3]] multiplicatio fieri potest per quodlibet separatim, at non si dividas, v.g. a / (b  c) nunquam\n[[405.4]] finito numero quantitatum, ubi tantum simplex divisor exprimes. Alterum\n[[405.5]] malum est quod nulla potest fieri additio et subtractio inter surda et integra, vel surda\n[[405.6]] et surda, v.g. 1 - Rq2, vel Rq3 - Rq2; si inde fieri posset Rq ex binomio educenda,\n[[405.7]] id saltem bonum foret, v.g. si ex Rqa - Rqb fieri posset Rq, c - d. Ita enim statim\n[[405.8]] quadrando ipsum et alterum terminum aequationis eliminarentur omnes quantitates\n[[405.9]] surdae, cum alias ex Rq3 - Rq2 non possint eliminari surdae, si enim ducantur in se\n[[405.10]] fiet 3  2 - 2Rq6. Quae si rursus quadrentur, fiet 25  24 - 4Rq150 et si rursus\n[[405.11]] quadrentur, fiet 2401  2400 - 8Rq, 2401 x 150. Quodsi aliquando contingeret\n[[405.12]] multis istis multiplicationibus perveniri aliquando casu ad numerum exacte quadratum,\n[[405.13]] tunc poterit ex radicibus pluribus surdis fieri una, sed ea multorum (graduum)\n[[405.14]] v.g. quadrato-quadrata etc. Et hoc secretum Analyseos foret definire, quando hoc fieri\n[[405.15]] possit, et forte datis quibusdam radicibus surdis adjungere tertiam ita comparatam, ut\n[[405.16]] omnes simul fiant summabiles, ita restabit solum una Radix surda addita in altera\n[[405.17]] parte aequationis. Observatio est maximi momenti. Dicamus: Rq9 - Rq4; inde fieri\n[[405.18]] potest Rq, 4  9 - 2Rq36 = Rq, 13 - 2Rq36. Caeterum exemplum alibi exhibui\n[[405.19]] occasione circuli, ubi duae Radices simul una sola exhibentur, ipsis aequivalente, etsi\n[[405.20]] alioquin sint irreducibiles, et occasione talium exemplorum methodos ejusmodi cogitare\n[[405.21]] opus est.\n[[405.22]] Quod dixi de divisione per binomia non procedente etiam sic exprimi potest,\n[[405.23]] quod scilicet ex data ratione partium non potest colligi ratio totorum. At ille in Analysi\n[[405.24]] quoque defectus est insignis quod saepe scire non possumus an non eo usque\n[[405.25]] variari queat aequatio, additione, abjectione, multiplicatione etc. ut plene fiat reducibilis\n[[405.26]] seu divisibilis. Et fortasse omnis aequatio in rei veritate est reducibilis ad\n[[405.27]] planam, si nos sciremus quousque ^nexaltariö variarique necesse sit in eum finem.\n[[405.28]] Denique multa sunt problemata infinitorum quae cognita hactenus analysi solvi\n[[405.29]] non possunt, ut summam invenire infinitarum linearum crescentium, ut Rq1, Rq2,\n[[406.1]] Rq3, etc., at tales summas invenire possumus per lineas, ita illam quoque differentiam\n[[406.2]] inter has lineas, etc. Ita quadrans quoque circuli certo modo linearum ejusmodi\n[[406.3]] summa est. Ita ipsae curvae lineae; ut alibi observavi. Talium propositionum quae\n[[406.4]] non analysi sed sola synthesi Geometrica condi possunt, fieri debet catalogus, ut\n[[406.5]] appareat quomodo interim saltem per lineas ista possint solvi, quemadmodum plures\n[[406.6]] duabus medias proportionales, quando problema planum non est, itidem per lineas\n[[406.7]] non pure Geometricas, id est ut ratio illa seu constructio ad rectam pura non detur,\n[[406.8]] exhibemus.\n[[406.9]] Ut continuis quas supra dixi quadrationibus etc. perveniatur ad numerum exacte\n[[406.10]] quadratum, necesse est tandem ita fieri ut alter alterum, vel uti est, vel divisus, vel\n[[406.11]] etiam per fractionem aliquam multiplicatus, metiatur. Nam si per numerum simplicem\n[[406.12]] multiplicatus metitur, jam tum metitur. Observavi enim Theorema notabile:\n[[406.13]] Si duo numeri in se invicem ducti faciunt quadratum, etiam se dividentes faciunt\n[[406.14]] quadratos. Quodsi jam ipsi non sunt quadrati, ut 18 et 2 qui faciunt 18 x 2 = 36:\n[[406.15]] Non poterunt esse quadrati dividentes 18 / 2 nisi se exacte metiantur. Sumamus tamen\n[[406.16]] exemplum 9 x 16, est quadratum = 144: Possunt dividentes facere quadratum etsi se\n[[406.17]] non metiantur, ut 18 / 32 (facit) 9 / 16. Si posset ergo in casu aliquo qualis propositus 3  2\n[[406.18]] ita dividi vel multiplicari per numerum quendam aliosque plures, integros vel fractos,\n[[406.19]] ut producti eius quadratum multiplicatum per 6 faciat quadratum, habebimus propositum,\n[[406.20]] et poterunt eliminari Radices surdae. Ergo opus est solutione huius problematis:\n[[406.21]] Datis duobus numeris, alium, unius eorum multiplicem reperire, cuius\n[[406.22]] quadratum in alterum ductum faciat quadratum. At malum in eo est, quod nunquam\n[[406.23]] quadratus ductus in non quadratum, facit quadratum. Imo non opus est quadratum\n[[406.24]] unius in alterum ductum facere quadratum, sufficit ipsum simpliciter in alterum\n[[406.25]] ductum facere quadratum. Ergo datis 3  2 - 2Rq6 quaeratur multiplex de 5 vel\n[[407.1]] cuius multiplex sit ipse 5 integer vel fractus, qui ductus in 6 producat quadratum.\n[[407.2]] Talis est ipse 6, ergo pro 5 substituatur 6, fiet 6 - 2Rq6. Imo error, antequam debeat\n[[407.3]] duci in 6 debet reddi quadratus. Recte ergo supra, et redit difficultas prior. Ubi ergo\n[[407.4]] numerus cuius quadratum ductum in 6 faciat quadratum, is numerus non potest\n[[407.5]] integer, nec potest esse quadratum, nisi per reductionem, v.g. 6 / 54 est quadratum etsi\n[[407.6]] non appareat, si ducas in 6 producetur 36 / 54, sed hoc pacto inferior postea non est\n[[407.7]] quadratum. Nunquam tota fractio est quadratum, nisi vel uterque vel neuter terminorum\n[[407.8]] sit quadratum. Ergo 6 multiplicetur per 1 / 54. Sed hoc non est quadratum, etsi\n[[407.9]] productum fiat quadratum. Ideo necesse est ut fractio data tam ante multiplicationem\n[[407.10]] quam post multiplicationem reduci possit ad quadratum, quod fortasse impossibile.\n[[407.11]] Huc ergo breviter reducitur problema: Dato numero non quadrato, invenire alium\n[[407.12]] quadratum, ita comparatum, ut his duobus numeris in se invicem ductis (vel per se\n[[407.13]] invicem divisis) productus sit quadratus. Si tale problema solvi posset, universaliter,\n[[407.14]] possemus semper plures radices surdas reducere in unam, et per consequens eliminare\n[[407.15]] omnino radices surdas.\n",
  "number": "40.",
  "title": "De imperfectione analyseos",
  "date": ""
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:40. De imperfectione analyseos 403.0;0:63:040:73 :L:l^. P. Fabri in Methodo: inventum Analyticorum priorum Aristotelis nulli Algebrae postponendum. P. Fabri in Methodo ait regulam Galilaei (aequali tempore acquiri motu naturaliter accelerato spatium idem quod acquiritur motu aequabili, cuius velocit...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>